 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Authorizes and directs the New York state department of health, to conduct a study on the high incidence of asthma in the borough of the Bronx in the city of New York and to prepare a remedial plan.",
 "title": "Authorizes a study on the high incidence of asthma in the borough of the Bronx in the city of New York and to prepare a remedial plan",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S3103",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S3103-2017",
     "printNo": "S3103",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 219\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n \nSenate Bill Number 3103, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to direct the New York State Department of Health to conduct\n      a  study  on  the  high incidence of asthma in the boroughs of the\n      Bronx, Manhattan, and Brooklyn in the city  of  New  York  and  to\n      prepare a remedial plan\"\n \nSenate Bill Number 5559 entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to direct the New York state department of health to conduct\n      a  study on the high incidence of asthma in the borough of Manhat-\n      tan in the city of New York and to prepare a remedial plan\"\n \nSenate Bill Number 5770 entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to direct the New York state department of health to conduct\n      a study on the high incidence  of  asthma  and  related  pulmonary\n      disorders  in  the borough of Brooklyn in the city of New York and\n      to prepare a remedial plan\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  These bills would require the State Department of  Health  to  conduct\nthree separate epdemiological studies related to the incidence of asthma\nin  Manhattan,  the  Bronx,  and  Brooklyn. The Department would also be\ntasked with implementing remedial plans to address their findings.\n \n  The Department is currently implementing a comprehensive  Preventative\nHealth agenda to make New York the healthiest state in the nation, which\nincludes  addressing  asthma  in  high incidence districts such as those\nidentified in the pending bills. Developed in collaboration with  multi-\nple  stakeholders,  the agenda will seek to improve the health status of\nall New Yorkers and to reduce health disparities  through  an  increased\nemphasis  on  prevention. Although these bills would appear to align the\nDepartment's goals, they do not address the concerns I raised last  year\nwhen I vetoed the same bill for Bronx County. (Veto No. 262 of 2016).\n \n  First,  these  bills  focus  solely  on  one-off  local  public health\nsolutions to combatting asthma, which does not align  with  the  Depart-\nment's  statewide  focus  on  this  issue.  For  example, the Department\ncurrently implements  two  statewide  programs:  the  Children's  Asthma\nProgram    and  the  Childhood  Asthma Coalitions Program. Through these\nprograms, the Department conducts outreach and education,  and  develops\nand  implements  plans  to  address  asthma's  prevalence throughout the\nState.\n \n  Second, these bills would require the Department to conduct three  new\nstudies  without  any  established  funding  source.  These  bills would\nrequire the collection of individual-level data from residents in  three\ncounties,  as  well  as  an  evaluation of every medical facility in the\nBronx, Manhattan, and Brooklyn to assess and  treat  patients  suffering\nfrom  asthma.  The cost of conducting all three studies and implementing\n \nremedial plans is estimated at $3 million. Such funding decisions should\nbe addressed in the context of the State budget negotiations. For  these\nreasons, I am constrained to veto these bills.\n \n  These bills are disapproved.                (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
